ield to defend you  in the past you won't find the shoddy  shield you discover in blood-borne to be  of much use to you the game demands  mobility avoidance is imperative lest a  group of demon dogs tear your flesh  apart while leaving you powerless to  slash your way out of danger yet  slashing away as a compelling response  due to blood-borne health regain system  should you take damage you have a  limited time to gain some of that lost  vitality by landing hits of your own the  system may strike you is incompatible  with the rhythms of typical Souls combat  but it's a wonderful fit in blood-borne  you see your foes are just as agile even  the Hulk's that descend upon you in  blood-borne x' boss battles boss arenas  are typically large affording you plenty  of room to escape the wrath of the  horror staring you down and affording  that horror the same luxury circle  strafing can no longer be your deep  strategy when the enemy proves more  limber than you these might seem like  small adjustments to the formula but  they changed the way you applied  fundamental skills like when and when  not to lock on to your enemies you can  carry a firearm in your left hand though  it makes for a poor offensive weapon  that's not just because it's tuned more  for staggering enemies than for damaging  them but also because ammunition is a  finite resource there's no spell  spamming equivalent in blood-borne  though a rifle or blunderbuss makes a  fine accompaniment to whatever blade you  wield in your right hand that melee  weapon by the way is transformable a  cleaver for instance is great for doing  close rapid damage but it's secondary  mode extends its reach at the cost of  speed and transforming during active  battle delivers a third type of attack  as the weapon morphs from one state to  the other  as expected blood-borne can be brutal  not as hard as Dark Souls or demon souls  but a step above Dark Souls 2 in its  level of difficulty as a result mastery  requires properly r